1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,888
Two sorted linked lists become
one. No new nodes. No copying.

2
00:00:05,938 --> 00:00:07,750
Only the arrows change.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:11,127
Merge two sorted linked lists.

4
00:00:11,177 --> 00:00:16,990
You are given the heads of two lists,
a and b. Each one is already sorted.

5
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:21,883
Return a single sorted list that
contains every node from both.

6
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:25,771
The catch: you must
reuse the existing nodes.

7
00:00:25,821 --> 00:00:28,661
The only field you may change is next.

8
00:00:28,711 --> 00:00:31,550
So the extra space has to be constant.

9
00:00:32,567 --> 00:00:33,986
Both lists are sorted,

10
00:00:34,036 --> 00:00:38,863
so the smallest value that is left is
always at the front of one of them.

11
00:00:38,913 --> 00:00:42,203
So the brute force way is:
compare the two fronts,

12
00:00:42,253 --> 00:00:45,877
copy the smaller one into a
brand new list, and repeat.

13
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:51,947
That is already linear time: big O of
n plus m, for lists of length n and m.

14
00:00:51,997 --> 00:00:56,440
But every node gets copied, so
the extra space is linear too.

15
00:00:56,490 --> 00:01:00,270
And that is exactly what
the problem does not allow.

16
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:03,191
So here is the better idea.

17
00:01:03,241 --> 00:01:06,260
Keep exactly the same
walk, but stop copying.

18
00:01:06,310 --> 00:01:11,510
Take the node itself, and link it into
the result by changing a next pointer.

19
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:16,243
But which node becomes the head of
the result? We do not know yet.

20
00:01:16,293 --> 00:01:21,663
So we start with a placeholder, called a
dummy node, and build the result behind it.

21
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:27,270
A reference called tail always points
at the last node of the merged list.

22
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:32,390
Everything from dummy to tail is merged
and sorted. That is the invariant.

23
00:01:34,533 --> 00:01:38,446
In code: dummy and tail
start on the same new node.

24
00:01:38,496 --> 00:01:42,563
Then the loop runs while
both lists still have nodes.

25
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:47,112
Compare the fronts: two
and one. One is smaller.

26
00:01:47,162 --> 00:01:51,850
So tail's next is set to that
node, and b moves forward.

27
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,311
Then tail steps onto
the node we just linked.

28
00:01:55,361 --> 00:01:57,910
The merged list is now one node long.

29
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:03,969
Next: two against five. Two is
smaller. Watch the arrow out of one.

30
00:02:04,019 --> 00:02:10,843
It used to point at five. Now it points
at two. No node moved. Only next changed.

31
00:02:11,267 --> 00:02:13,083
Five against five: a tie.

32
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:18,158
The comparison is less than or equal,
so the node from a goes first.

33
00:02:18,208 --> 00:02:23,457
That keeps the merge stable: equal
values stay in their original order.

34
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:30,170
Now five, from b, against eight. Five
is smaller. Link it, and advance.

35
00:02:31,400 --> 00:02:35,478
Eight against nine. Eight
is smaller. Link it.

36
00:02:35,528 --> 00:02:37,990
And now, list a has run out.

37
00:02:38,433 --> 00:02:41,305
The loop stops as soon
as one list is empty.

38
00:02:41,355 --> 00:02:45,954
The rest of the other list is still
sorted, and still linked together.

39
00:02:46,004 --> 00:02:50,602
So a single assignment attaches all
of it: tail's next becomes a or b,

40
00:02:50,652 --> 00:02:52,063
whichever one is left.

41
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:57,750
Finally, skip the placeholder. Return
dummy's next. That is the real head.

42
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:02,950
Drawn in a straight line, it is the
same seven nodes, now in sorted order.

43
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:07,977
Time: every pass of the loop
links exactly one node.

44
00:03:08,027 --> 00:03:13,770
So the time is linear: big O of n plus
m, the two lengths added together.

45
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:19,904
Space: one dummy node and a few
references, whatever the size of the input.

46
00:03:19,954 --> 00:03:22,090
That is constant extra space.

47
00:03:23,733 --> 00:03:25,006
Where is this used?

48
00:03:25,056 --> 00:03:27,443
It is the merge step of merge sort.

49
00:03:27,493 --> 00:03:31,203
And merge sort is the usual
way to sort a linked list.

50
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:37,542
It is also the building block for merging
k sorted lists: merge them in pairs,

51
00:03:37,592 --> 00:03:38,503
or use a heap.

52
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:40,204
And the dummy node?

53
00:03:40,254 --> 00:03:43,160
It removes edge cases
far beyond this problem.

54
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:47,143
That story has its own short
video, linked in the description.

55
00:03:48,967 --> 00:03:50,211
Thanks for watching.

56
00:03:50,261 --> 00:03:54,807
Subscribe to Edge Cases for more
Python, algorithms, and system design,

57
00:03:54,857 --> 00:03:56,037
explained visually.
